<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="ceramide de novo biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: Ceramides Ceramides| belong to a diverse class of lipids called sphingolipids that have important biological properties and control many aspects of cellular metabolism |CITS: [22248339]|. They are composed of |FRAME: SPHINGOSINE| linked to |FRAME: Fatty-Acids| via an amide bond. Ceramides make up a large percentage of the cell membrane and are the major lipid constituent of lamellar sheets in the intercellular spaces of the stratum corneum |CITS: [12553851]|. These lamellar sheets provide the barrier property of the epidermis. Besides having structural properties, ceramides can act as a second messengers in the sphingomyelin cycle |CITS: [8106344]|, and also play a role in cell aging, apoptosis and differentiation |CITS: [10652518]|. There are three major pathways for ceramide generation. This pathway describes &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; biosynthesis. &lt;b&gt;About this Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The defining step in the &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; biosynthesis of ceramides is the pyridoxal-5&apos;-phosphate-dependent condensation of |FRAME: PALMITYL-COA| with |FRAME: SER| catalyzed by |FRAME: CPLX66-305 &quot;serine palmitoyltransferase&quot;| |CITS: [9363775]|. The resultant |FRAME: DEHYDROSPHINGANINE| is immediately reduced to |FRAME: CPD-13612| in an NADPH dependent reaction catalyzed by |FRAME: HS04306-MONOMER|. |FRAME: MONOMER66-34367 &quot;Dihydroceramide synthase&quot;| catalyzes the acylation of sphinganine to yield |FRAME: CPD3DJ-82|, followed by a desaturation at the 4,5 position to form |FRAME: Ceramides| |CITS: [9312549]|. The last two steps of the pathway were decided by the discovery of the fungal secondary metabolite |FRAME: CPD-13181| |CITS: [8850625]|. Fumonisin B1 is a potent inhibitor of the N-acylation of sphingoid bases and blocks the pathway at the |FRAME: MONOMER66-34367 &quot;dihydroceramide synthase&quot;| step. This clarified that the latter part of the pathway proceeds first by acylation and then desaturation instead of vice versa |CITS: [1860857]|. The pathway occurs in the endoplasmic reticulum.</Description>
